# Local agent rules

These rules apply to this project and can be committed with it.

## Always do this first

- Before making changes, read this file and follow it.

## Working agreement

- Do not modify files unless I explicitly ask you to.
- Present the solution first. Make changes only after I approve or explicitly request implementation.
- Reading files, searching the repo, and running non-mutating checks is allowed when needed.
- Use the shortest, simplest, cleanest approach that solves the request.
- Prefer small, safe changes.
- Keep changes scoped to the request. No drive-by refactors.
- When adding code, do not add comments explaining it.
- If you notice unrelated issues, mention them but do not change them unless asked.
- Do not add secrets, credentials, tokens, or local environment files.
- Do not create commits, amend commits, or change git history unless I explicitly ask.

## Code style

- Prefer arrow functions for new JavaScript and TypeScript code.
- Do not use arrow functions when they are technically inappropriate, such as object methods, constructors, generators, or code that requires its own `this`, `arguments`, or `super`.
- Before creating a new function, check whether an equivalent function already exists.
- Use clear, descriptive function and variable names that explain what they represent or what the function does.
- Avoid vague names such as `data`, `item`, `value`, `handle`, `process`, or `doThing` unless the surrounding context makes the meaning obvious.
- Follow the existing structure and architecture of the project when it aligns with the good practices described in this file.
- Do not preserve bad patterns only for consistency. If the existing structure conflicts with these rules, mention the conflict and choose the safer, cleaner approach.
- Do not use the `any` type in TypeScript.

## Verification

- Before running any verification command, ask me first.
- Recommend the smallest relevant check available, such as a targeted test, typecheck, lint command, or build command.
- Run the check only after I approve it.
- If no check is available, say so.
- If a check fails because of unrelated existing issues, report that clearly.

## When unsure

- Say that you are unsure.
- If I ask you to choose anyway, pick the least risky option, implement it, and verify with a quick check if available.